# tools/r/rye_harness_roster_witness.rish -- a harness that assembles its paths must roster the
# directory it assembles them from.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. tools/p/parity_ch01.rish builds and runs 116 Rye programs, and every one of their
# paths exists only while the harness runs. The harness holds `let dir = "rye/tests"` and a list of
# 116 bare stems, and reaches each program as `${dir}/${s}.rye`, so the tree carries the directory
# and the stems and leaves the join to the shell.
#
# Two consequences follow, and both are quiet. A census reading for filenames calls all 116 unbuilt
# -- one did on 20260906, and its count reached the operator card as a fact. And a program added to
# rye/tests/ while the list stays as it was is run by nothing, while the suite still reports GREEN,
# because the suite asserts over the list it holds rather than over the directory it names. That
# second failure is open, silent, and looks exactly like success.
#
# The tree already keeps this rule in two other rooms -- crypto_tool_declaration_scan.sh reds a
# `ghost_module`, module_roster_scan.sh reds an `unrostered` file -- and each stops short of the
# largest assembled site in the tree. This rung carries the same rule to every harness that builds
# a path out of a directory and a stem.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal.
#   Every stem a harness names resolves to a .rye file in that harness's own directory.
#   Every .rye file in that directory is named by a stem, so nothing stands there unrun.
#   These behaviors on real pen trees. Each is pinned by its own assert below and counted by the
#   control in `cases=` rather than totalled here, since a total carried inside a sentence is a
#   number a later landing falsifies -- and this one already was.
#
#   The agreeing harness free and reading zero. A stem naming nothing, counted and refused and
#   named. A program nobody lists, the same three ways. A seam symlink read as a program from both
#   sides. An empty corpus refused rather than read clean. The build-site census telling a literal
#   target, a continuation line, and prose apart. The unresolved ratchet proven at its ceiling and
#   one over. A harness that builds and one that only reads, told apart by one line. Both spellings
#   of a build, shell and Rishi. The three site classes proven disjoint. And `--paths` spelling
#   every stem's real path, marking its answer, refusing an empty tree while still marking it, and
#   refusing a flag it has never been taught.
#
# WHAT IS REPORTED RATHER THAN GATED. Three readings. The build-site census, telling a literal
# target from an assembled one. `unresolved`, the assembled shapes this scan reads past. And
# `assemblers_not_harnesses`, the scripts that spell a `.rye` path and hand it to nothing.
#
# A guard that reds for its own blindness is a guard somebody turns off, so the blindness rides as a
# ratchet whose ceiling only ever falls. It fell 10 -> 1 on `20260906`, when the reading was narrowed
# to its own subject. Ten of the eleven shapes under the elder ceiling build nothing at all: six
# roster guards comparing a directory against a page, three pens planting a corpus, one witness
# census resolving a subject's path in order to read it. Each is honest work, so each is counted in
# the open.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. That a listed program compiles, that it asserts anything, or that running it
# is worth the seconds. Only that the set the harness names and the set on disk are one set.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/rye_harness_roster_witness.rish

say "harness-roster: Language EN -- a path that exists only while a script runs is a path no reader can check."
say "harness-roster: Style Gauge, Meter -- the counts come first and the reason follows them."
say "harness-roster: Lens TAME -- bound everything: the harness names a set, and the set is checked."

let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rye_harness_roster_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "harness-roster: a harness names a program that is not there, or leaves one on disk unrun -- read the scan's own lines"
say scan.out
assert scan.out contains "stems_absent=0" else "harness-roster: every stem must name a program that stands on disk"
assert scan.out contains "files_unlisted=0" else "harness-roster: every program in a harness's directory must be named by a stem, or nothing runs it"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "harness-roster: the scan must balance before it is believed"
say "field GREEN -- the set a harness names and the set on disk are one set"

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rye_harness_roster_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "harness-roster: the control must complete"
say control.out
assert control.out contains "1 free" else "harness-roster: an agreeing harness must pass free"
assert control.out contains "2 free" else "harness-roster: a clean pen must read zero rather than merely pass"
assert control.out contains "4 free" else "harness-roster: the reading must find the harness it is meant to find"
assert control.out contains "5 free" else "harness-roster: the units must be counted off the harness's own list"
assert control.out contains "6 bitten" else "harness-roster: a stem naming no file must be counted"
assert control.out contains "7 bitten" else "harness-roster: a stem naming no file must be refused"
assert control.out contains "8 bitten" else "harness-roster: the refusal must name the path, so the repair is one line away"
assert control.out contains "9 bitten" else "harness-roster: a program no stem names must be counted"
assert control.out contains "10 bitten" else "harness-roster: a program no stem names must be refused -- this is the silent failure"
assert control.out contains "11 bitten" else "harness-roster: the refusal must name the file nobody runs"
assert control.out contains "12 free" else "harness-roster: a seam symlink listed by its stem is a program and passes"
assert control.out contains "13 bitten" else "harness-roster: a seam symlink no stem names must be refused too"
assert control.out contains "14 bitten" else "harness-roster: an empty corpus must refuse rather than read clean while measuring nothing"
assert control.out contains "15 free" else "harness-roster: a literal build target must be read as literal"
assert control.out contains "16 free" else "harness-roster: a target on a continuation line must still be read"
assert control.out contains "17 free" else "harness-roster: prose after the driver name must not be counted as a site"
assert control.out contains "18 free" else "harness-roster: the residue must be published rather than dropped"
assert control.out contains "19 free" else "harness-roster: the unresolved ratchet must pass free at its ceiling"
assert control.out contains "20 bitten" else "harness-roster: the unresolved ratchet must refuse one over its ceiling"

# The residue NAMED beside its count. The census that consumes --paths grants its credit only when
# the assembling script compiles something, and until this landed the residue rode as a bare number
# -- so a reader could not tell a doubt that reaches that census from one that cannot (the residue row `20260906.113130`).
assert control.out contains "21 free" else "harness-roster: --paths must name the site it could not read"
assert control.out contains "22 free" else "harness-roster: the named site must be counted too, so the two readings agree"
assert control.out contains "23 free" else "harness-roster: --paths must mark that this copy names its residue"
assert control.out contains "24 free" else "harness-roster: the naming marker must ride even where there is nothing to name"
assert control.out contains "25 free" else "harness-roster: a tree with no residue must read zero rather than merely stay quiet"

# --paths, the answer the compile-reach census reads. Pinned by ordinal like every case above, so a
# phase that is dropped or reordered reds here rather than going quiet.
assert control.out contains "26 free" else "harness-roster: --paths must spell the path a stem stands for"
assert control.out contains "27 free" else "harness-roster: --paths must answer for every stem, not the first"
assert control.out contains "28 free" else "harness-roster: --paths must count what it printed"
assert control.out contains "29 free" else "harness-roster: --paths must mark its answer, or a silent one reads as an empty tree"
assert control.out contains "30 bitten" else "harness-roster: --paths on a harnessless tree must refuse"
assert control.out contains "31 bitten" else "harness-roster: an empty answer must still carry its marker ahead of the refusal"
assert control.out contains "32 bitten" else "harness-roster: an unknown flag must refuse rather than fall back to counting"

# A HARNESS BUILDS. Spelling a `.rye` path is not what makes a script a harness -- handing that path
# to the rye driver is. The two pens hold the same script under the same name and differ in one
# line, so the reading is attributable to the rule rather than to anything else that moved.
assert control.out contains "33 free" else "harness-roster: a script that assembles a path and builds nothing must not be counted unresolved"
assert control.out contains "34 free" else "harness-roster: it must be counted in the open instead, never silently dropped"
assert control.out contains "35 free" else "harness-roster: and named, so a decline a reader cannot see is a decline nobody can question"
assert control.out contains "36 free" else "harness-roster: the same shape handed to the driver must be counted unresolved"
assert control.out contains "37 free" else "harness-roster: and must leave the non-harness count, since one script is one class"

# Both spellings. Shell writes `rye/bin/rye build x.rye`; Rishi writes the same call as a quoted
# list. The elder program stripped quotes from the driver token and not from the verb, so it read
# the first and walked past the second -- 47 sites, and the count grows as shell molts to Rishi.
assert control.out contains "38 free" else "harness-roster: a Rishi-spelled build must be read as a build site"

# The three site classes are disjoint. An assembled target ending in `.rye` matched both patterns
# and was counted twice, and the leftover absorbed the difference as if it were prose.
assert control.out contains "39 free" else "harness-roster: an assembled target must be counted as assembled"
assert control.out contains "40 free" else "harness-roster: and not a second time as literal"
assert control.out contains "41 free" else "harness-roster: so the residue stands at zero rather than going below it"

assert control.out contains "control_failures=0" else "harness-roster: every planted case must read the way its label says"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "harness-roster: the control must agree with itself"
say "RED and GREEN paths proven -- every planted refusal bitten and every honest reading left free, counted by the control in cases="

say "GREEN: harness-roster -- a harness that assembles its paths rosters the directory it assembles them from."
